# Definitions, anticipated questions and background notes. Status ai-draft / draft throughout.
# A definition's `at` names the first sentence in reading order that uses the term, and a few words of it that occur
# verbatim in the node (the build checks them); mentions of the term and its aliases are marked in the text
# automatically. Aliases are true synonyms only: inflections and the paper's own spellings.

DEFS = [
    # ---- the setting
    {"id": "vector-model", "term": "vector model", "aliases": ["vector models"],
     "html": r"A theory of $N$ scalar fields $\phi_I$ rotated as a vector by a $U(N)$ symmetry ($O(N)$ for real fields), studied in its singlet sector. The free model has no interactions; the critical model is the infrared fixed point reached from it by a quartic interaction $(\sum_I|\phi_I|^2)^2$, for $2<d<4$.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p1", "quote": "the critical vector model"}},
    {"id": "singlet", "term": "singlet sector", "aliases": ["singlets"],
     "html": r"The part of the theory invariant under $U(N)$: operators built from $\sum_I\phi_I^*\phi_I$ and its derivatives, and the states they create. Only this sector has a gravity dual. On flat space or a sphere one simply restricts to it; at finite temperature imposing it requires integrating over a holonomy.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p1", "quote": "singlet sector"}},
    {"id": "high-spin", "term": "high-spin gravity", "aliases": ["higher spin gravity", "higher-spin gravity"],
     "html": r"A gravitational theory with a massless field of every integer spin $J$, the graviton at $J=2$ among them, whose interactions are tied together by an infinite-dimensional gauge symmetry. Classically it is described by Vasiliev's equations. Here its coupling is $G_N=1/N$, and the paper's bulk theory is conjectured to be a gauge-fixed form of it.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p1", "quote": "high-spin gravity theory"}},
    {"id": "non-local", "term": "non-local",
     "html": r"Of an action: containing couplings between fields at separated points, not only products of fields and finitely many derivatives at one point. The bulk theory here has non-local interactions; a choice of field normalizations makes its quadratic part local.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p1", "quote": "non-local bulk theory"}},
    {"id": "bilocal", "term": "bi-local field",
     "aliases": ["bi-local fields", "bilocal field", "bilocal fields", "bilocals", "bi-locals"],
     "html": r"A field of two spacetime points. Here $G(x_1,x_2)=\frac1N\sum_I\phi_I^*(x_1)\phi_I(x_2)$, the $U(N)$-invariant product of the scalars, its fluctuation $\eta=N(G-G_0)$ around the free propagator, and the multiplier $\Sigma(x_1,x_2)$. Every singlet operator is built from $G$, so it is the natural variable to map to the bulk.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p1", "quote": "bi-local field"}},
    {"id": "tt", "term": "transverse traceless", "aliases": ["traceless transverse"],
     "html": r"Of a symmetric tensor field: divergence-free and with every trace zero. These conditions keep only the physical polarizations of a massless spin-$J$ field, so the bulk fields $\Phi_J$ are high-spin gauge fields in a fixed gauge.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p1", "quote": "transverse traceless spin"}},
    {"id": "off-shell", "term": "off-shell",
     "html": r"Holding for every field configuration in the path integral, not only for solutions of the equations of motion. An off-shell map is a change of integration variables between field theory and bulk, so it relates the full path integrals, quantum effects included, and not only classical solutions.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p2", "quote": "explicit off-shell mapping"}},
    # ---- finite N
    {"id": "lagrange", "term": "Lagrange multiplier",
     "html": r"A field introduced to impose a condition: integrating over it produces a delta function. Here the bilocal $\Sigma(x_1,x_2)$ imposes that $G$ equals the scalar bilinear. Keeping it as a field, rather than integrating it out, is what makes the bilocal theory exact at finite $N$.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p3", "quote": "is a Lagrange multiplier"}},
    {"id": "syk", "term": "SYK model",
     "html": r"The Sachdev–Ye–Kitaev model: $N$ Majorana fermions with random couplings among a few of them at a time, solvable at large $N$. Its standard treatment rewrites it, after averaging over the couplings, in terms of a bilocal $G(t_1,t_2)$ and a multiplier $\Sigma(t_1,t_2)$; the paper applies this “$G$–$\Sigma$” formalism to vector models.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p3", "quote": "in the SYK model"}},
    {"id": "constraints", "term": "finite $N$ constraints",
     "html": r"Relations that the bilocal $G$ obeys only because it is built from $N$ vectors: as a matrix it has rank at most $N$, so the determinant of any $(N+1)\times(N+1)$ matrix $G(x_i,y_j)$ vanishes. They are invisible order by order in $1/N$, and the earlier large-$N$ bilocal integral ignores them.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p3", "quote": "finite $N$ constraints"}},
    {"id": "auxiliary", "term": "auxiliary field", "aliases": ["auxiliary fields"],
     "html": r"A field with no dynamics of its own, whose path integral imposes a condition or produces an interaction. Here mostly the multiplier $\Sigma$ and its bulk image $\tilde\Phi_J$, which impose the finite-$N$ constraints; the critical model's Hubbard-Stratonovich field $\sigma$ is one too.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p3", "quote": "auxiliary field"}},
    {"id": "lattice", "term": "lattice", "symbol": r"$V$",
     "html": r"The paper's simplest regulator: space replaced by $V$ points, so that a bilocal is a $V\times V$ matrix and the continuum is $V\to\infty$. It cuts off both short and long distances. In the finite-temperature section the same letter $V$ denotes instead a spatial volume.",
     "at": {"node": "largeN-p1", "quote": "on a lattice of $V$ points"}},
    {"id": "empty-ads", "term": "empty AdS", "aliases": ["empty AdS space"],
     "html": r"AdS with every high-spin fluctuation set to zero, $\Phi_J=0$, which corresponds to the bilocal sitting exactly at its large-$N$ value $G_0$. Its amplitude is the path-integral weight of that configuration; at finite $N$ it must vanish, because $G_0$ has rank larger than $N$.",
     "at": {"node": "finiteN-p1", "quote": "the amplitude of empty AdS"}},
    {"id": "principal", "term": "principal series",
     "html": r"The unitary representations of the Euclidean conformal group $SO(d+1,1)$ with complex dimension $\Delta=d/2+is$, $s$ real. Partial waves with these dimensions and every spin $J$ form a complete basis for normalizable functions of two points, which is what lets the bilocal be expanded and matched to bulk fields.",
     "at": {"node": "adscftmap-p1", "quote": "principal series"}},
    {"id": "shadow", "term": "shadow",
     "html": r"The shadow of an operator of dimension $\Delta$ has dimension $\tilde\Delta=d-\Delta$ and the same spin, and pairs with it in a conformally invariant way. The multiplier $\Sigma$ transforms like a product of two shadows of the free field, of dimension $(d+2)/2$, so its expansion is the shadow version of $\eta$'s.",
     "at": {"node": "adscftmap-p3", "quote": "bulk shadow coefficient"}},
    {"id": "field-redef", "term": "field redefinitions",
     "html": r"Changes of variables in the bulk path integral that leave the physics unchanged. Here they are the normalizations $f_{\Delta,J}$ and $\tilde f_{\Delta,J}$ of each conformal component, not fixed by symmetry; the paper chooses them so that the bulk coupling of $\Phi_J$ to $\tilde\Phi_J$ is local, the Jacobian is one, and the quadratic action is local.",
     "at": {"node": "adscftmap-p3", "quote": "field redefinitions in the bulk"}},
    {"id": "hs", "term": "Hubbard-Stratonovich",
     "html": r"An auxiliary field $\sigma$ that turns a quartic interaction into a quadratic coupling: integrating over $\sigma$ in $\frac12\sigma\sum_I|\phi_I|^2-\frac N{4\lambda}\sigma^2$ gives back a quartic interaction of strength proportional to $\lambda/N$. The critical vector model is the limit $\lambda\to\infty$, reached at long distances.",
     "at": {"node": "critical-p1", "quote": "local Hubbard-Stratonovich auxiliary field"}},
    # ---- finite volume
    {"id": "sphere-F", "term": "sphere free energy",
     "html": r"$F=-\log Z$ for the theory on the round sphere $S^d$, one of the simplest observables of a CFT; in odd $d$ its finite part is universal and counts degrees of freedom. Here it is the regulated $Z_\phi^N$ of $N$ free fields, a normalization the paper fixes explicitly.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p4", "quote": "the sphere free energy is reproduced"}},
    {"id": "hyperbolic", "term": "hyperbolic space",
     "html": r"$\mathbb{H}^{d+1}$, the space of constant negative curvature, which is Euclidean AdS. In coordinates $ds^2=dy^2+\sinh^2y\,d\Omega_d^2$ its conformal boundary at $y\to\infty$ is the sphere $S^d$, so it is the bulk for the CFT on $S^d$, as the upper half-space is for $\mathbb{R}^d$.",
     "at": {"node": "section-3.1-p1", "quote": "dimensional hyperbolic space"}},
    # ---- finite temperature
    {"id": "saddle", "term": "saddle point", "aliases": ["saddle points", "saddle"],
     "html": r"A stationary point of the action that dominates a path integral when the action is large, here proportional to $N$. At large $N$ the bilocal integral is dominated by the free propagator $G_0$, and the thermal holonomy integral by one distribution of eigenvalues.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p5", "quote": "governed by a saddle point"}},
    {"id": "holonomy", "term": "holonomy",
     "html": r"The $U(N)$ matrix $U=\exp(i\int_0^\beta A_t\,dt)$ acquired by going once around the thermal circle in a background gauge field. Integrating over it projects the thermal trace onto singlets; its eigenvalue distribution tells the confined phase (uniform) from the deconfined one (all eigenvalues near one).",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p5", "quote": "the holonomy to the bulk"}},
    {"id": "confined", "term": "confined phase", "aliases": ["confining", "confined"],
     "html": r"The low-temperature phase of the large-$N$ singlet theory, where only low-dimension singlets matter and the free energy does not grow with $N$; the holonomy's eigenvalues are spread uniformly. It is the phase reached by taking $N$ large before the volume, and the one the paper maps to thermal AdS.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p5", "quote": "“confining” phase"}},
    {"id": "deconfined", "term": "deconfined phase", "aliases": ["deconfined"],
     "html": r"The high-temperature phase, where the singlet constraint barely matters and the free energy is that of $N$ free fields, of order $N$; the holonomy is close to the identity. It is reached by taking the volume large before $N$, and presumably corresponds to a black hole in the bulk, which the paper leaves open.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p5", "quote": "“deconfined” phase"}},
    {"id": "thermal-ads", "term": "thermal AdS",
     "html": r"Euclidean anti-de Sitter space with its Euclidean time made periodic, of period $\beta=1/T$, and no horizon. In AdS/CFT it is the bulk saddle of the low-temperature, confined phase, while a black hole describes the high-temperature one.",
     "at": {"node": "section-1-p5", "quote": "thermal AdS space"}},
    {"id": "images", "term": "sum over images",
     "html": r"The propagator on a circle obtained from the one on a line by adding its translates by every multiple of the circumference, possibly with phases. Here the holonomy's phases cancel all images except those at multiples of $N\beta$, which disappear at large $N$.",
     "at": {"node": "finiteT-p5", "quote": "sum over images"}},
    {"id": "clock", "term": "clock",
     "html": r"The “clock” matrix is diagonal with entries $e^{2\pi iI/N}$; the “shift” matrix moves each basis vector cyclically to the next. Both are holonomies with uniformly spread eigenvalues, related by a gauge transformation. With the shift, the $N$ scalars on a circle of length $\beta$ become one scalar on a circle of length $N\beta$.",
     "at": {"node": "finiteT-p7", "quote": "“clock” matrix"}},
]

QUESTIONS = [
    {"id": "q-notation", "at": {"node": "finiteN"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What do $N$, $V$, $d$, $\eta$ and $\Sigma$ stand for?",
     "a": r"$N$ is the number of complex scalars, and $G_N=1/N$ is the bulk coupling. In Sections 2 and 3, $V$ counts the points of the lattice regulator, so the continuum is $V\to\infty$; in Section 4 the same letter is a spatial volume. $d>2$ is the boundary dimension, the bulk is $AdS_{d+1}$, and the map takes different forms for $d>4$ and $d<4$. $\eta=N(G-G_0)$ is the bilocal's fluctuation, and $\Sigma$ the multiplier that fixes $G$.",
     "answeredBy": ["largeN-p1", "finiteN2-p1", "finiteT-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-why-fail", "at": {"node": "largeN"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why is the original map not valid at finite $N$?",
     "a": r"Because a bilocal built from $N$ vectors is a matrix of rank at most $N$, and the large-$N$ bilocal integral does not know this. Its weight $|\det G|^{N-V}$ converges only for $V\le N$, so the continuum limit exists only if $N$ is taken to infinity first. Order by order in $1/N$ the constraints are invisible; beyond perturbation theory they matter, for instance for the amplitude of empty AdS.",
     "answeredBy": ["largeN-p2", "finiteN2-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-no-loops", "at": {"node": "finiteN2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why does the finite-$N$ theory have no loop diagrams?",
     "a": r"The quadratic action pairs $\eta$ with $\Sigma$ and gives $\eta$ a propagator, but $\Sigma$ has none with itself. All vertices come from $W[\Sigma]$ and involve only $\Sigma$'s. A loop would need two $\Sigma$'s contracted together, so none exists: each connected correlator of $\eta$'s is a single tree diagram, which reproduces the free-field contractions directly. The same holds in the bulk for $\Phi_J$ and $\tilde\Phi_J$.",
     "answeredBy": ["finiteN2-p2", "bulkAction-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-tilde-phi", "at": {"node": "bulkAction"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What are the new bulk fields $\tilde\Phi_J$?",
     "a": r"They are the bulk image of the multiplier $\Sigma$: a second tower of transverse traceless spin-$J$ fields, coupled to the $\Phi_J$ through the local term $\int\tilde\Phi_J\Phi_J$ and interacting among themselves through $W$. They are auxiliary: integrated out order by order in $1/N$, they give back the earlier bulk action. Keeping them gives a consistent theory at finite $N$, because they impose the bulk version of the finite-$N$ constraints.",
     "answeredBy": ["bulkAction-p1", "bulkAction-p2", "section-1-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-empty-ads", "at": {"node": "bulkAction"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "What does the vanishing amplitude of empty AdS mean?",
     "a": r"Empty AdS, all $\Phi_J=0$, corresponds to $G=G_0$, the free propagator, which as a matrix on $V$ lattice points has rank $V$. A bilocal built from $N<V$ vectors cannot have that rank, so the measure must vanish there. The exact computation gives the naive large-$N$ factor times a product $\prod_k(k(N-k+1))^{V-k+1}$, which vanishes for integer $N<V$. In the continuum at finite $N$, empty AdS has zero amplitude.",
     "answeredBy": ["bulkAction-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-topology", "at": {"node": "section-1"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Does the finite-$N$ bulk theory need a sum over geometries or topologies?",
     "a": r"No. Even at finite $N$ the field theory maps to fields on one fixed AdS background, because the map is a change of variables in the path integral, and the finite-$N$ effects are carried by the auxiliary fields $\tilde\Phi_J$ instead. The paper suggests this is presumably a special feature of high-spin gravity theories; it does not discuss what replaces it in theories closer to Einstein gravity.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-1-p3", "adscftmap-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-vasiliev", "at": {"node": "bulkAction"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Is the bulk theory Vasiliev's high-spin gravity?",
     "a": r"That is a conjecture, not shown here. With $\tilde\Phi_J$ integrated out perturbatively, the bulk action has the physical field content of Vasiliev's theory, and it was conjectured to be a gauge-fixed form of it on a fixed AdS background. Its kinetic terms are quartic in derivatives, so it carries extra off-shell modes, conjectured to be related to those of high-spin gravity. The paper uses no specific property of Vasiliev's equations.",
     "answeredBy": ["bulkAction-p2", "section-1-p1", "footnotes"]},
    {"id": "q-critical", "at": {"node": "critical"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "How does the critical vector model appear in the bulk?",
     "a": r"Through a boundary condition. The Hubbard-Stratonovich field $\sigma$ couples to $\eta(x,x)$, which the map relates to the leading boundary falloff of the scalar $\Phi_0$. At the infrared fixed point, $\lambda=\infty$, integrating over $\sigma$ sets that falloff to zero off-shell, so $\Phi_0$ starts at $z^{d/2}$, like the other spins, rather than at $z^{d-2}$. This mirrors how double-trace flows act in standard AdS/CFT.",
     "answeredBy": ["critical-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-sphere", "at": {"node": "section-3.2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Where does the sphere free energy come from on the gravity side?",
     "a": r"From a prefactor. The fields $\Phi_J$ appear linearly, so integrating them out forces $\tilde\Phi_J=0$, and what remains is $e^{NW_\text{bulk}[0]}=Z_\phi^N$, the constant term of $W$ copied from the field theory. No classical action and no loop diagram contributes. Writing this constant in bulk language would need a bulk regularization, which the next subsection takes up.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-3.2-p3", "section-3.2-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-oneloop", "at": {"node": "section-3.2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "How does this compare with earlier one-loop tests of higher-spin holography?",
     "a": r"Without an action those tests could not compute the order-$N$ term, but they computed the one-loop term assuming canonical two-derivative kinetic terms for each spin. For $U(N)$ it vanished as expected; for $O(N)$ it equalled one real scalar's free energy, read as a shifted coupling $G_N\propto1/(N-1)$; other examples allowed no such shift. The bulk action here is quartic in derivatives, so the paper calls the $U(N)$ match a coincidence and suggests no shift.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-3.2-p5", "section-3.2-p6", "section-1-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-reg", "at": {"node": "regs"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why is it enough to match mode counts rather than the regulators themselves?",
     "a": r"Both regulated theories keep $SO(d+1)$, and the map respects it, so it splits into blocks, one for each $SO(d+1)$ representation. If both sides keep equally many modes in every representation, each block is square, and a generic square block is invertible. One can then restrict the CFT-to-AdS map to the surviving CFT modes, or the inverse map to the surviving bulk modes; both approach the continuum map as the cutoffs are removed.",
     "answeredBy": ["regs-p5", "regs-p6"]},
    {"id": "q-order-limits", "at": {"node": "finiteT"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why does the order of the large-$N$ and large-volume limits matter?",
     "a": r"The singlet theory has a transition at $T^{d-1}V\simeq N$, with $V$ here the spatial volume: below it the singlet bilocals dominate, with free energy of order $T^{2d-1}V^2$; above it the order $NV$ deconfined modes do. Taking $N$ large first keeps $T^{d-1}V\ll N$ at any temperature, the confined phase; taking the volume large first gives the deconfined phase. Only the former is mapped to the bulk.",
     "answeredBy": ["finiteT-p2", "finiteT-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-deconfined", "at": {"node": "finiteT"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why can't the same map describe the high-temperature phase?",
     "a": r"There the dominant holonomy is the identity, so each scalar is periodic and the bilocal is invariant under shifting either time argument by $\beta$ separately, not only both together. With the zero-temperature map it is unclear what this extra periodicity imposes on the bulk fields. The paper expects a different, black-hole-like geometry and a different map, as the non-vanishing traces of the holonomy also suggest, and leaves this open.",
     "answeredBy": ["finiteT-p11"]},
    {"id": "q-not-shown", "at": {"node": "section-1"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "What does the paper not show?",
     "a": r"It does not perform the integral over $\Sigma$ or $\tilde\Phi_J$ beyond perturbation theory, nor show that the bulk theory is Vasiliev's. Its regularization fixes mode counts, not explicit regulators suited to computation, and bulk boundary conditions for high-spin fields stay subtle. At finite temperature it maps only the confined phase, at leading order in the large-$N$ saddle; holonomy fluctuations at finite $N$, and the deconfined phase, are left open.",
     "answeredBy": ["finiteN2-p3", "regs-p7", "finiteT-p10", "finiteT-p11"]},
]

# Eight notes; four are shown to the default reader (the duality, the G-Sigma trick, the harmonic analysis behind the
# map, and the holonomy), the others to students only. Two sit in the introduction; the others follow the first body
# paragraph that works with the idea. Listed in reading order.
BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-hs-duality", "after": "section-1-p1", "title": "Vector models and the high-spin duality", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>In a vector model the singlet operators of lowest dimension are bilinears $\sum_I\phi_I^*\partial^J\phi_I$, one for each spin $J$. In the free model those with $J\ge1$ are conserved currents, so the theory has an infinite tower of conserved high-spin currents. In AdS/CFT a conserved spin-$J$ current is dual to a massless spin-$J$ gauge field, so the gravity dual of the free model's singlet sector must be a high-spin gravity, with a massless field of every spin and the graviton at $J=2$. Klebanov and Polyakov proposed this duality in 2002 for the critical $O(N)$ model and Vasiliev's theory in $AdS_4$, the free model differing only by the boundary condition of the bulk scalar. Singlet correlators factorize at large $N$, so the bulk is weakly coupled, with $G_N\sim1/N$. Unlike most holographic dualities, both sides are then tractable, which is why an explicit map from one to the other seems within reach.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-gsigma", "after": "section-1-p3", "title": "The G–Σ formalism in the SYK model", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>The Sachdev–Ye–Kitaev (SYK) model has $N$ Majorana fermions $\chi_i(t)$ with random couplings among $q$ of them at a time. After averaging over the couplings, the action depends on the fermions only through the bilocal $G(t_1,t_2)=\frac1N\sum_i\chi_i(t_1)\chi_i(t_2)$. One inserts a delta function setting $G$ equal to this bilinear, written as an integral over a Lagrange multiplier $\Sigma(t_1,t_2)$, and integrates out the fermions, which now appear only quadratically. The result is an exact action, $N$ times a functional of $G$ and $\Sigma$ containing $\log\operatorname{Pf}(\partial_t-\Sigma)$. At large $N$ its saddle-point equations are the Schwinger–Dyson equations, but nothing in the rewriting assumed large $N$. This $G$–$\Sigma$ formalism is what the paper applies to the vector model, where $\Sigma$ has usually been integrated out; keeping it retains the finite-$N$ information.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-collective", "after": "largeN-p2", "title": "Collective fields and why the bilocal is classical", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>The collective field method of Jevicki and Sakita rewrites a theory of $N$ fields in terms of invariant composites, here the bilocal $G(x_1,x_2)$. The change of variables has a Jacobian, a power of $\det G$, and the action becomes a function of $G$ alone. Both are proportional to $N$ at large $N$, so $N$ multiplies the whole bilocal action and $1/N$ plays the role of $\hbar$. The bilocal is then classical at large $N$: its saddle point is the exact large-$N$ two-point function, here the free propagator $G_0$, and fluctuations around it are suppressed by powers of $1/N$. For free fields the $1/N$ expansion of singlet correlators is exact and simple, so the many interactions of the collective field must cancel order by order. A collective field is not an independent field, though: it inherits constraints from its definition, which a large-$N$ expansion never sees.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-harmonic", "after": "adscftmap-p1", "title": "Harmonic analysis on the conformal group", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>Functions of two points that transform like a product of two operators of dimension $\Delta_0$ can be expanded in conformal partial waves, much as functions on a line are expanded in plane waves. This is harmonic analysis on the Euclidean conformal group $SO(d+1,1)$, worked out by Dobrev, Mack, Petkova, Petrova and Todorov. Each partial wave is a three-point structure $\langle\mathcal O_{\Delta_0}\mathcal O_{\Delta_0}\mathcal O_{\Delta,J}\rangle$, labelled by a spin $J$ and a dimension $\Delta=d/2+is$ on the principal series, and together they form a complete orthogonal basis. The dimension $d-\Delta$, the shadow, labels the same representation. In AdS the same labels classify solutions of the wave equation: a spin-$J$ field of mass $M^2=\Delta(\Delta-d)-J$ has bulk-to-boundary propagators of dimension $\Delta$. Expanding both sides over one group and matching them representation by representation is the core of the map.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-double-trace", "after": "critical-p1", "title": "Boundary conditions and double-trace flows", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A bulk scalar of mass $m^2$ in $AdS_{d+1}$ behaves near the boundary as $z^{\Delta_-}$ or $z^{\Delta_+}$, with $\Delta_\pm=\frac d2\pm\sqrt{\frac{d^2}4+m^2}$. When both falloffs are allowed there are two consistent boundary conditions, dual to an operator of dimension $\Delta_-$ or $\Delta_+=d-\Delta_-$. Deforming the CFT by a double-trace term $f\,\mathcal O^2$, with $\mathcal O$ of dimension $\Delta_-$, starts a renormalization group flow whose infrared end has $\mathcal O$ of dimension $\Delta_+$; in the bulk the flow only changes the boundary condition, as Witten and Gubser and Klebanov showed. In the free vector model $\sum_I|\phi_I|^2$ has dimension $d-2$, and the quartic interaction is exactly such a double-trace deformation; at the critical point the scalar has dimension 2. The paper finds the same mechanism off-shell, as a constraint on the boundary falloff of $\Phi_0$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-sphere-F", "after": "section-3.2-p1", "title": "The sphere free energy of a CFT", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>For a CFT on the round sphere $S^d$, the free energy $F=-\log Z$ is a natural observable: the sphere has no scale besides its radius, and $F$ depends on it only through ultraviolet divergences. In odd $d$ the finite part is universal, and in $d=3$ the $F$-theorem says that it decreases along renormalization group flows, so it counts degrees of freedom. A free complex scalar gives about $0.13$ in $d=3$, and $N$ of them give $N$ times that. In holography the leading term, of order $N$ here, is the classical bulk action, and the corrections of order one come from one-loop determinants of all bulk fields. In high-spin gravity this one-loop sum runs over infinitely many spins and needs a regularization, usually with zeta functions. The paper finds instead that in its finite-$N$ formalism no bulk term contributes to the sphere free energy at all.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-holonomy", "after": "finiteT-p1", "title": "Singlet projection and the holonomy", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>To keep only $U(N)$ singlets in a thermal trace, one averages the action of the group over $U(N)$, which is the singlet projection. In the Euclidean path integral this is a gauge field $A_t$ on the thermal circle, constant in space, whose only gauge-invariant content is the holonomy $U=e^{i\int_0^\beta A_t\,dt}$; the scalars become periodic up to this twist. Integrating over the eigenvalues $e^{i\theta_k}$ of the holonomy gives a matrix integral which, at large $N$, is dominated by one distribution of the $\theta_k$ on the circle. If it is uniform, every $\operatorname{Tr}U^n$ with $n\ne0$ vanishes and only singlets are counted: the confined phase. If it is localized near $\theta=0$, the traces are of order $N$ and the system behaves almost like $N$ free fields: the deconfined phase. These traces, Polyakov loops, are the order parameters.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-hawking-page", "after": "finiteT-p2", "title": "Thermal AdS and the Hawking–Page transition", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A CFT at temperature $T$ on a sphere is dual to gravity in a spacetime whose boundary is $S^{d-1}\times S^1_\beta$. Two smooth Euclidean geometries fill it in: thermal AdS, global AdS with periodic Euclidean time, and the Euclidean AdS black hole, in which the thermal circle shrinks to zero at the horizon. Thermal AdS has a free energy of order one, the black hole one of order $1/G_N$. Hawking and Page found that the black hole dominates above a temperature of order the inverse AdS radius, and Witten identified this Hawking–Page transition with deconfinement in large-$N$ gauge theories: the Polyakov loop vanishes in thermal AdS but not in the black hole. In vector models the transition sits instead at $T^{d-1}V\sim N$, and the paper maps only the thermal AdS side.</p>"""},
]
